en:
Heh heh heh...
Just as I thought.
Then naturally, you always use the stairs.{p}

"Ms. Akiyama...
This morning, I found this."
On the paper the girl held out as she said that,{p}

Oh my, have you forgotten already?
You weren't supposed to know the board existed.
...So how did you know what it was called?{p}

Heh heh heh...
You knew about the board all along, but you answered that you didn't, isn't that so?
...See, you did lie.{p}

Oh, you know it?
...That's a little surprising.
Well, never mind.{p}

Heh... but still...
Who on earth started calling it 'the Board of Death,' I wonder?
It's a loathsome name, isn't it.{p}

Here on the frame...
You can see words carved into it, can't you?
At Iwashita's urging, I peered closely at the board.{p}

As if in prayer, I raised the camera once more.
...Please!
At that instant...☆{p}

Back there... in the instant the light flashed before my eyes.
I clearly saw the word that had been too scratched up to read.
It was...{p}

'To deepen the ... between students. The Graduating Class'
Part of it can't be read, but you have to get a clear shot of that.
"All right..."{p}

If you put yourself in Eto's place, it must have been painful all the same.
Everyone was staring at her test.
And it was hardly a score anyone would praise...{p}

You've only just started here, so you wouldn't know, but every year an abnormal number of people from this school die.
Accidents, crimes, suicides...
All of them ill-omened deaths, aren't they?{p}

First, on a math test, you solve the problems and hand it in without writing your name.
That much is easy, isn't it?
But that test has to be a perfect hundred.{p}

Click-click-click...
Anyway, it looks like I'd better move on to the next one, fast.
Um, next is...{p}

...Well, fine.
You did apologize, at least.
As promised, I'll get back to the story."{p}
